explanation.md:
A C source file is translated by a compiler into a program the machine can execute. The entry point is `main`; `#include <stdio.h>` makes declarations for standard input/output available; `printf` writes text and `scanf` reads values. A small program has a predictable shape:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int count;
    if (scanf("%d", &count) != 1) return 1;
    printf("%d\n", count);
    return 0;
}
```

The format specifier must match the pointed-to type: `%d` with `int *`, `%ld` with `long *`, `%lld` with `long long *`, and `%lf` with `double *` for `scanf` (whereas `printf` uses `%f` for a `double`). Check that `scanf` read what you expected when input may be malformed. The beginner tasks specify valid input, but the habit matters.

Use `if`/`else` when a decision selects one path; use `switch` when one expression is matched against several constant cases. A `for` loop is convenient when a counter has a clear start and end; a `while` loop fits a condition that controls repetition. In every loop, identify three things before you run it: the initial state, the condition that keeps it going, and the update that makes progress.

```c
long long total = 0;
for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {
    total += i;
}
```

The classic off-by-one error is choosing `< n` when the problem includes `n`, or `<= n` when it does not. Trace the first and last iteration on paper. In C, zero is false and every nonzero integer is true; comparison operators produce either 0 or 1.

A function gives a named operation a clear input and result. Its declaration (prototype) tells callers the parameter and return types before the body is seen. C passes ordinary arguments by value: the function receives a copy. Returning a result is usually the simplest way to communicate a calculation back.

```c
int square(int value);

int square(int value) {
    return value * value;
}
```

When a function must change the caller's object, pass its address as a pointer. This is not a separate pass-by-reference feature: the pointer value is copied, and dereferencing that copy accesses the same object. Keep each function focused so it is possible to test and reason about one operation at a time.

An array holds several values of one element type in adjacent storage. Its valid indices are `0` through `length - 1`; C does not check whether an index is in range. An invalid index is not a harmless mistake: it can read or overwrite another object's memory. Keep the length beside the array and make loops respect it.

```c
int values[4] = { 8, -2, 5, 11 };
for (int i = 0; i < 4; ++i) {
    printf("%d\n", values[i]);
}
```

An array is not a resizable list. When an array is passed to a function, the parameter behaves as a pointer to its first element, so the function also needs the length. This is one reason the DSA unit will make node links and allocation explicit.

A `struct` makes one value out of related fields that may have different types. The `.` operator accesses a field through a struct object; `->` accesses it through a pointer to a struct.

```c
struct Reading {
    int value;
};
struct Reading sample = { .value = 12 };
struct Reading *where = &sample;
printf("%d\n", where->value);
```

A struct describes a shape; it does not allocate a variable by itself. A local struct variable has automatic storage duration, while a struct obtained through `malloc` must be released with `free`. This distinction prepares you to understand a linked-list node without asking the pointer syntax to do magic.

